In article <1991Aug30.154508.1891@ecl.psu.edu> s1y@ecl.psu.edu writes:
>	If you are a Barbarian, you could name your two handed sword Orcrist
>and get the same effect as the Artifact (found out from the spoiler file).
>Wonder if you could cheat a shopkeeper like that ? Orcrist defends well
>against orcs.

Actually, naming a two-handed sword "Orcrist" MAKES it the artifact.
For proof, try it again with a second sword.  This is one of two
"do it yourself" artifacts, the other is to name an elven dagger "Sting".
Which brings me to one of my pet peeves: Where is Glamdring ?
Orcrist without Glamdring is like Biter without Beater (so to speak).

And while we're on the subject of named weapons, I some suggestions
for the dev team.  Most named weapons are not particularly powerful.
Grimtooth, Cleaver, Ogresmasher, et al are generally not worth the
pack space.  If these have come from an altar, I'll generally keep
offering til I get Mjollnir or Stormbringer, although Frost Brand
and Fire Brand are decent.  If there isn't an altar, Excalibur is 
makeable with the help of a fountain, so this is often the named
weapon of choice.

I guess my complaint is that weapons like Demonbane and Werebane
are just ordinary swords except for when fighting the creature
that the sword has a special attack on.  Even then, they are
no better than Frost Brand or Fire Brand (double damage).  As a
result, the majority of named weapons have no more interest or
use than the million and one flavors of hafted weapons that
somebody wasted code on.

Perhaps the time has come to add some punch to the lesser named
weapons, in order to make them useful and worth playing with.
At the same time, a few other policy changes might add more
variety and not have every game feature Stormbringer or Mjollnir.

1) When your god grants you a weapon, prevent another weapon
   from being granted, until the player has advanced at least
   two experience levels, or dispatched a certain number of
   monsters (ideally, with the granted weapon).

2) Stop giving out the crummy stuff.  No more Sting or Orcrist
   from my lawful deity (I try to make them myself if I get
   the chance, to speed my way to Mjollnir).  Anything that
   comes from a deity ought to be pretty good.

3) If the lesser named weapons are still interesting, let them still
   be found laying around (like now).  Getting Cleaver real early
   is occasionally helpful to a character that doesn't even have
   a sword yet.

4) Demonbane, Ogresmasher, Trollsbane, Giantslayer, and Werebane
   should be beefed up so that against most creatures they cause
   more damage than any conventional weapon (e.g. two-handed sword),
   but less than FB/Mojo etc.  HOWEVER, against the creature they
   were forged for, they should be nothing less than awesome.
   One hit should have a good chance of killing the creature, and
   they should always induce fear into the creature.

5) Sunsword should be brought up to just below Fire/Frost Brand
   against normal, very strong against undead, and should blind
   creatures when it strikes them.  Sunsword should provide 
   immunity from fear of ghosts.

6) Demonbane should provide immunity from the sickness attacks
   of Demogorgon and Juiblex.  It should also provide immunity
   from fear when a Demon is summoned.

7) A troll killed with Trollsbane should lose the power to resurrect.

8) Dragonbane should be beefed up, and should kick dragon's butts.
   When standing over the dragon's corpse, you should be able to
   "A"pply it.  "There is a gray dragon corpse here, skin it?".
   Say yes, and you just made a suit of dragon scale mail.
   Of course, "Dragonbane" should really have been "Tailbiter", right?

The above suggestions would encourage people to use whatever named
weapon they were fortunate enough to get, thus adding a bit of variety.


